Certainly! Here's a JavaScript program utilizing advanced features like async/await, Promises, and destructuring:

```javascript
async function fetchData(url) {
  const response = await fetch(url);
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP error! status: ${response.status}`);
  return await response.json();
}

const processAndMergeData = async (urls) => {
  try {
    const promises = urls.map(url => fetchData(url));
    const data = await Promise.all(promises);

    return data.reduce((acc, current) => {
      const [firstKey] = Object.keys(current);
      acc[firstKey] = current[firstKey];
      return acc;
    }, {});
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error fetching data:', error);
  }
};

(async () => {
  const urls = [
    'https: 
    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1'
  ];

  const mergedData = await processAndMergeData(urls);
  console.log('Merged Data:', mergedData);
})();
```

This program asynchronously fetches data from multiple URLs, processes the results using JavaScript's advanced asynchronous features, and merges the data into a single object.